<section class="home-section research-section">
	<p class="home-eyebrow">What I am exploring</p>
	<h2>Research interests</h2>
	<ul class="research-list">
        <li>Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning</li>
        <p>I am most interested in its applications to competitive games like Chess and Poker. Rather than treating a model as a black box which simply 
            produces an action, I am interested in understanding how models represent positions, evaluate possible decisions, and learn strategies. While machine 
            learning may be capable of generating near optimal moves, I find the process of understanding why a model makes a decision just as interesting as the 
            decision itself.
            </p><br>
        <li>Deep Reinforcement Learning</li>
        <p>Machine Learning often comes with the caveat that a good model requires lots of data and labels, however this data may be hard to get or we may 
            not even know what the correct label is. Deep Reinforcement Learning is an intriguing solutions for this, it allows the agent to explore the 
            environment and learn, however we run into the One Armed Bandit problem, where we must determine how much we want the agent to explore the environment 
            and how much we want it to capitalise on its strategy. We also run into the problem of sample efficiency, as our model changes it must gather new 
            data to evaluate its change in policy. I am particularly interested in how we can make this learning process more efficient while still discovering strong 
            policies. 
        </p><br>
        <li>Decision Making Under Uncertainty</li>
        <p>Partially observable Markov decision processes (POMDPs) are applicable to many real world scenarios, whether it be Tennis, Poker or financial markets, 
            the way we make decisions under uncertainty and potentially noisy information is incredible, especially when combined with the need for quick 
            execution. Designing and creating models which make justified decisions to optimise their chance of success and do so within the allotted time is a fascinating 
            challenge, and one which I am very interested in exploring further. 
        </p><br>
        <li>Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning</li>
        <p>What's more interesting that seeing a model adapt, several models adapting! This is the most fascinating case to watch as models interact and 
            improve together by beating each other, with intriguing outcomes as to whether there is a dominant model or they keep adapting to each other. 
            These algorithms can be used to simulate group behaviour and allows us to see what strategies emerge when agents compete, cooperate, betray one 
            another, or learn to forgive previous behaviour, potentially discover strategies that would be difficult to obtain from fixed training data.
        </p><br>
        <li>Computational Game Theory</li>
        <p>Computational game theory provides a rigorous framework to the study of strategic decision making in competitive environments, with it  providing rational
            solutions to problems when the agents are self-interested and offering solutions which provide a useful theoretical foundation for understanding competitive 
            reinforcement learning and self-play.
        </p><br>
        <li>Geometric Deep Learning</li>
        <p>It is interesting to see how the structure of data can be incorporated into the design of machine learning models. It gives us a rigorous mathematical basis to 
            derive CNNs, GNNs, Transformers and more, using symmetry, invariance and equivariance. It also provides insight into which models are best suited 
            for certain data representations. I find the connection between mathematical structure and model architecture particularly interesting.
        </p><br>
    </ul>
</section>
